#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:1
msgid "What is Polymorphism?"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:3
msgid ""
"In a dynamic language like Python, we don't have to do anything special to "
"allow a function to accept arguments of different types:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:11
msgid "\"hello\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:15
msgid ""
"But Rust's static type system requires us to specify the types of our "
"functions' arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:20
#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:52
msgid "\"{}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:25
msgid "// print_value(\"hello\"); // ❌ Mismatched types!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:31
msgid ""
"If you are coming from a dynamic language like Python, the concept of "
"polymorphism may be new to you because in dynamic languages all functions "
"are implicitly polymorphic. But in Rust, functions only accept arguments of "
"the exact types they expect, and we must use a mechanism like generics to "
"support polymorphism."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:37
msgid ""
"Static typing is a powerful tool that allows the compiler to enforce correct "
"usage of our APIs: If your function needs to be given an `i32` in order to "
"function correctly, the compiler won't allow a user of that function to pass "
"in a string."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:42
msgid ""
"But static typing is restrictive in cases where we want to write code that "
"is flexible in its handling of types. In the example on this slide, we might "
"want our `print_value` function to be able to print both numbers and "
"strings, but as written it can only accept `i32` values."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/python.md:47
msgid ""
"Edit `print_value` to make it generic using `Display` to print different "
"kinds of values:"
msgstr ""
